Confirmed the bug. The loop now is:

```
for nt:
  buf = nt&1
  prefetch nt+1 into buf^1 buffer   // overwrites the buffer that was READ in iteration nt-1!
  wait<1>; sync
  compute on buf
```

The prefetch at iteration nt writes into buffer (nt+1)&1 = 1-buf. That buffer was read during iteration nt-1 (as its compute buffer). There's no __syncthreads between the last read of that buffer (iteration nt-1 compute) and the prefetch write (iteration nt top). So a fast thread could issue the cp.async writes to buffer 1-buf while a slow thread is still reading it from iteration nt-1. That's the race.

Fix: add a __syncthreads() at the END of the loop body (after compute, before next iteration's prefetch). Classic double-buffer structure:

```
for nt:
  wait; sync          // tile nt ready
  compute tile nt
  sync                // all reads of tile nt done
  prefetch nt+1 (into other buffer); commit
```

That's the standard FA2 pattern: sync at end protects the overwrite. Cost: 2 syncs per iteration — same as before (old code had one sync after wait + one at end).

Let me restructure: move prefetch to end of loop body.

```
for (nt...) {
  if (nt==0) { /* initial prefetch already done before loop */ }
  wait; sync;
  compute;
  sync;
  prefetch nt+1; commit;
}
```

Wait — the initial prefetch of tile 0 was done before the loop. Then inside: wait<1> (tile0 ready, tile1 flying)... hmm let me redo cleanly:

